{"answer":"The supplied results do not directly compare “different fine-tuning methods” for how they affect the utility of vector representations specifically for legal technical documents across diverse domains. Instead, the papers address related but different questions: fine-tuning for legal dense retrieval (embedding utility), and fine-tuning strategies for other legal NLP tasks (e.g., legal machine translation), or for long-document domain adaptation that does not establish practical utility.

For legal dense retrieval/embeddings, [1] fine-tunes multilingual law embedding models with contrastive objectives (monolingual and bilingual) on a multilingual EU environmental legislation corpus, reporting improved Top-k retrieval accuracy and transfer to unseen languages, with gains attributed to enhanced language-independent content-level representations. [2] compares training setups for a dense retriever (no fine-tuning vs legal-only vs mixed legal + question-answer supervision), finding legal-only is better on specialized tasks while mixed training is more robust across retrieval types and question-based search. [6] presents a benchmark that enables studying retrieval across heterogeneous Brazilian legal domains and includes a domain-adapted dense embedding model fine-tuned on JUÃƒÂ¡-aligned supervision, but it does not isolate which fine-tuning methods change representation utility.

For other adaptation settings, [4] continues pretraining a Russian ModernBERT variant on legislative documents and reports reduced masked-token prediction loss, but it does not isolate contribution of context or establish practical legal utility; [3] studies full fine-tuning vs LoRA/QLoRA vs zero-shot prompting for unfair ToS clause detection (not vector utility for legal technical documents across domains); [5] focuses on fine-tuning vs from-scratch for legal machine translation (not document embedding utility); [8] uses synthetic data with parameter-efficient fine-tuning for German legal question answering (not an embedding utility comparison across domains); [7] compares embedding methods under noisy labels for Czech decisions (not fine-tuning method comparison); [9] and [10] are classification/prompting and small-model reasoning studies, respectively.","grounded":false}
